A||||5||dr fritz||2.8 to 3.1||||5||
Z||000000||dr fritz||05-31-2001||12:45 AM||||hullo all<BR>im new here.... i have a 2.8 v6   i am more than just thinking of stroking it to a 3.1.  has anyone done this yet? (im sure the answer is yes) what thoughts or advice do you have.  also.... any online addy's with good info on this topic would be greatly appreciated.   <BR>thanx <P>ps this will be the first of many questions i will be asking..... im restoring and improving my 86gt this summer||||reg||5
Z||000001||FLASHY||05-31-2001||02:38 AM||||First of all, welcome dr fritz!<P>I know some will want to tell you to do a search on this topic but I personally know that Voytek has done this recently (as others have done it too). Since I knew where to look I've added a few links to help get you started. Hope that helps.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009642.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009642.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009759.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009759.html</A> <P>good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||05-31-2001||10:06 AM||||I did it last fall, it worked out decently, with ported manifold and new exhaust about to go on, it's definately a step up from the stock (expecially when you rods are almost shot and you have a valve bouncing off the piston). But if I had it to do over again, I'd just get a 3.4 short or long block. I don't have my work sheet in front of me, but the 2.8-3.1 rebuild (I had the machine shop do all the work) wound up at $1800, including the new crank, cam, lifters, 1 new rod, resizing the other 5, pistons, blue printing, assembly, etc. And since it was a 'performance' rebuild they only gave me a 1 month warrenty. all in all, I could have gotten a 3.4 long block and had the starter holes bored for probrably $500 or so less, without having to wait for the shop to finish the rebuild. if you want anymore info, email me at wayrb@manpower.usmc.mil<p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Steven||05-31-2001||10:45 AM||||I am doing the change on mine now, and have done a lot of work myself. I have made a couple of mistakes along the way. The big one is ordering a kit for the 91-92 Firebird to get the correct pistons for a 3.1 with iron heads. The kit came with an oil pump, which I upgraded to a high volume pump. What I did not know, was that the 3.1 motors use a pump with a bigger pick up. The pump I got was a Melling part # M134-HV. What I needed was a # M95-HV. Also you might want to do a search on Pistons. The best one so far that I read was written by 88Formula. As for if I would do it again...nope. 3.4 long block next time.<P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dave Gunsul||05-31-2001||11:04 AM||||I'm in the process of doing this right now.<BR>If the cam comes in today (the one I bought had the wrong cam in the box) I will be picking the completed 3.2 with 10:1 comp.<BR>pistons tonight.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Guess I won't be getting much sleep before I leave for the dells tomarrow morning.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000005||88formula||05-31-2001||09:23 PM||||I have covered this subject to death with performance data from Dyno2000 and Dragstrip2000. I know what your thinking but I get really accurate results for the GM 60-degree v6 engine using these programs because I have so much information on them including about 150 camshaft prophiles. Here is the thread I did about compression ratios a while back. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008285.html</A>  I think that building the 2.8 is the way to go unless you like lots of torque and you don't mind spending a little more money than the 3.4 can't be beat. <P>Do a search on this subject and you'll be reading for days. <P>Steven, about the camshaft thing, I didn't get back to you because I had some major problems with the guy who was doing my machine work. It delayed the whole project and I had to do a lot of things over again. I also lost a lot of money and that also delayed the 3.4 project. When I do get the engine back I will degree the camshaft and post the results. I did do a little research on it and I think you might be right. That may be the same camshaft that Crane puts out. The degree wheel will tell all! <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-31-2001).]||||reg||1
